(require-doc "lib")

(page "lang" "lang"
  (p "Loads the rest of the library and defines the two forms a package uses to split its "
     "code across files. Both resolve paths inside the package whose file is currently being "
     "evaluated, so a mod's " (code "(require \"player/weapons\")") " reads the mod's own "
     (code "scripts/player/weapons.s7") ", never the base game's.")

  (section "Forms"
    (fn-table
      (fn "(require path)"
          "Loads " (code "scripts/<path>.s7") " from the current package. "
          "Raises an error when no package is loading or the file is missing. Returns " (code "#t") ".")
      (fn "(require-data path)"
          "Loads " (code "data/<path>.s7") " from the current package, with the same errors. "
          "Returns " (code "#t") ".")))

  (section "Usage"
    (p "A base game's " (code "init.s7") " is typically a list of requires:")
    (code 'scheme "
      (require \"flow\")
      (require \"player/weapons\")
      (require \"player/hud\")
      (require-data \"things\")")
    (note "Both forms only work while a package script or data file is loading. Calling "
          (code "require") " from a hook such as " (ref "tick") " raises an error, because "
          "there is no current package at that point."))

  (section "What it loads"
    (p "After defining the two forms, " (code "lang.s7") " requires " (page-ref "lists") ", "
       (page-ref "math") " and " (page-ref "ui") " in that order. The engine binds "
       (ref "package-load-script") " and " (ref "package-load-data") " before this file "
       "runs; " (code "require") " is a thin wrapper that supplies the package id from "
       (ref "current-package-id") ".")))
